<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Asia (Roman province)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/133-bce">133 BCE</a>–7th century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Asia was a Roman province in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, formed from the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-iii">Attalus III</a> bequeathed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 133 BCE. Organised by 129 BCE, it was among the richest provinces of the empire, with cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, its leading city and the governor's seat, Smyrna and Pergamon. It was an early centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, home of the Seven Churches of the Book of Revelation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asia-roman-province.jpg" alt="Asia (Roman province)" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Asia_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> was a Roman province covering most of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> crushed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> at Magnesia in 190 BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-apamea">Treaty of Apamea</a> in 188 BCE gave most of the territory to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalid-dynasty">Attalid dynasty</a> of Pergamum. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-iii">Attalus III</a>, a close ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> without a legitimate heir, bequeathed his kingdom to Rome. On his death in 133 BCE the pretender <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristonicus">Eumenes III</a> rebelled, and the consul Marcus Perperna defeated him. Manius Aquillius then formally established the province. It first took in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysia">Mysia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troad">Troad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeolis">Aeolis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> and Caria, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> islands except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-gracchus">Gaius Gracchus</a> in 123 BCE gave members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equites">equestrian order</a> the right to collect taxes in Asia. Communities that could not pay borrowed from Roman lenders at exorbitant rates and often lost their land. By 88 BCE Mithridates VI of Pontus had conquered virtually all of Asia and ordered the slaughter of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and Italians in the province, with estimates of the dead from 80,000 to 150,000. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> defeated him and in 85 BCE reorganized the province into eleven assize districts, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, Pergamum and Smyrna, which competed for dominance.</p><p>Under Augustus it became a proconsular province and the most prestigious senatorial one. It was a system of hundreds of largely self-governing Greek cities that competed for status through festival games, cults and oratory, and it had no full legions. In the 3rd century the Antonine plague and the Gothic invasions of the 250s and 260s brought decline. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> divided the province into seven in the 4th century, and after the great plague of 543 many interior cities shrank to villages.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Asia (Roman province)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The chief city grew up on the east coast facing <b>Asia</b>, on the site of the modern town of Chios, while smaller communities, such as the fortified hilltop village excavated at Emporio, occupied the rest of the island.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its later greatness as the leading city of <b>Roman Asia</b>, a centre of early Christianity linked with the apostle Paul and the Gospel of John, and a council city of the church in 431 CE, rested on the foundations laid in these centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus describes the Lydians of Croesus’ day as the bravest and most warlike people in <b>Asia</b>, fighting on horseback with long spears.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Their chief town grew up on the south-east coast, on the site of modern Pythagoreio, with a natural harbour facing <b>Asia</b>, and Samos became one of the twelve members of the Ionian League, which met at the Panionion on Mount Mycale opposite.</blockquote><a href="/empire/samos">Samos · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its position controlled the crossing between Europe and <b>Asia</b> and the passage of ships to the Black Sea.</blockquote><a href="/state/abydos">Abydos</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus says that under Madyes, son of Protothyes, the Scythians defeated the Medes and dominated <b>Asia</b> for 28 years.</blockquote><a href="/state/ishkuza">Ishkuza</a></li></ul></section>
